+The Japanese row is no longer second-hand. The primary validation reports the
+same threshold of 3.44 that the meta-analysis tabulated, and adds the task, the
+Praat version and an AUC of 0.939 [@hosokawa2019abi]. The same first author
+published three Japanese papers: this ABI validation, an AVQI version 02.02
+validation [@hosokawa2017avqi], and an AVQI version 03.01 validation not yet in
+this reference's library.
+
+The South Indian validation ran its ROC analysis twice, against breathiness and
+against overall grade, and reports two thresholds: 3.66 with breathiness as the
+reference standard (AUC 0.816, sensitivity 61.73%, specificity 94.94%) and 3.41
+with overall grade (AUC 0.719, sensitivity 59.42%, specificity 79.19%)
+[@jayakumar2024abi]. The ABI targets breathiness, and the authors conclude it
+correlates better with breathiness than with grade, so the table above uses
+3.66. The 3.41 is noted here only so that it is not mistaken for the South
+Indian ABI cutoff. Sensitivity at 3.66 is low: the threshold maximises the
+Youden index for that retrospective sample of 160 recordings, but it misses
+nearly four breathy voices in ten [@jayakumar2024abi].
+
+Across all published language validations the ABI thresholds now run from 2.35
+in US English to 3.69 in Korean, with 2.68 Finnish, 2.94 Brazilian Portuguese
+(counting 1–20), 2.97 Persian, and 3.66 Malayalam and Kannada in between
